calib                package:AA                     R Documentation

Blood Plasma Data

Description:

     The 'calib' data frame has 27 rows and 2 columns.

     The data show the measurements of the concentration of an enzyme
     in  human blood plasma.  The true concentration 'x' is obtained
     using an  accurate but expensive laboratory method, while the
     measured concentration 'y' is obtained by a faster and less
     expensive automatic method.  The goal is to use the observed data
     pairs to estimate values of the true concentration based on
     further measurements using the less expensive method.

Usage:

     data(calib)

Format:

     This data frame contains the following columns:

     'x' the true expensive measurement;

     'y' the less expensive measurement.

Source:

     The data were obtained from

     Aitchison, J. and Dunsmore, I.R. (1975).  _Statistical Prediction
     Analysis_.  Cambridge University Press, Cambridge.   Page 183.

References:

     Brazzale, A.R., Davison, A.C. and Reid, N. (2007).  _Applied 
     Asymptotics: Case Studies in Small-Sample Statistics_.  Cambridge 
     University Press, Cambridge.  Example 7.2.

Examples:

     data(calib)
     plot(x ~ y, data = calib)
     mod <- lm(x ~ y, data = calib)
     abline(mod)
